{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response template --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response stamp --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response set --help;'","aggregated_output":"✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py template [-h] [-o OUTPUT]\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help           show this help message and exit\n  -o, --output OUTPUT\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py stamp [-h] [--harness HARNESS] [--model MODEL]\n                                [--scope SCOPE]\n                                [--kind {dataset,component,study,skill}]\n                                [--dependency DEPENDENCY] [--capture CAPTURE]\n                                response\n\npositional arguments:\n  response\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --harness HARNESS     e.g. \"claude-code (subagent)\"\n  --model MODEL\n  --scope SCOPE         what this assessment did and did not cover\n  --kind {dataset,component,study,skill}\n                        the object's kind (see references/project-kinds.md)\n  --dependency DEPENDENCY\n                        e.g. \"stamped-conventions@0.1.0\"; repeatable\n  --capture CAPTURE     hash a retained delivery manifest/archive; preserve\n                        any recorded Git commit\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py set [-h] --item ITEM\n                              --status {met,not-applicable,not-checked,partial,unmet}\n                              --reason REASON [--source SOURCE]\n                              [--evidence EVIDENCE] [--flag FLAGS]\n                              [--drop-evidence]\n                              response\n\npositional arguments:\n  response\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --item ITEM           item id, with or without the 'stamped-checklist:'\n                        prefix\n  --status {met,not-applicable,not-checked,partial,unmet}\n  --reason REASON\n  --source SOURCE       probe:<name>, judgement, probe:<name>+judgement,\n                        human:web-app\n  --evidence EVIDENCE   uri, or uri=description; repeatable\n  --flag FLAGS\n  --drop-evidence       clear the evidence already on this item (by default it\n                        is kept when --evidence is not given)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The single replay completed on the declared entrypoint with exit code 0. All 12 selected artifacts were recreated byte-for-byte, including the figures and numerical arrays; no comparison tolerance was needed. The replay helper reports no retrieval and no capture issues. I’m finishing the worker-stop practice/access assessment now, then I’ll validate the response and scientific record against the frozen validators and compare the inspection copy with its initial manifest."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' \\\"\"'$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_SOURCE/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\"; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response template -o response.json >commands/response-template.stdout 2>commands/response-template.stderr; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response stamp response.json --harness '\"'Codex (independent assessment)' --model 'GPT-6' --scope 'Complete worker stop delivery; STAMPED checklist, worker-declared access route, scientific adequacy, and one documented replay' --kind study --capture \\\"\"'$STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\" >commands/response-stamp.stdout 2>commands/response-stamp.stderr; cat response.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' \\\"\"'$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_SOURCE/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\"; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response template -o response.json >commands/response-template.stdout 2>commands/response-template.stderr; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response stamp response.json --harness '\"'Codex (independent assessment)' --model 'GPT-6' --scope 'Complete worker stop delivery; STAMPED checklist, worker-declared access route, scientific adequacy, and one documented replay' --kind study --capture \\\"\"'$STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\" >commands/response-stamp.stdout 2>commands/response-stamp.stderr; cat response.json'","aggregated_output":"# Response, report and stamp\n\nEvery STAMPED assessment produces four files (five with the optional fresh-rerun report), named for **the day the assessment was made** (UTC, `YYYY-MM-DD`) — not the object's commit date, and not the date of an earlier assessment being redone:\n\n| File | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `<date>-assessment.md` | the human report (layout below) |\n| `<date>-response.json` | the machine-readable per-item answer, with evidence and pins |\n| `<date>-probes.json` | what the scripted probes said, unedited, including the ones overridden |\n| `<date>-url.txt` | the checklist-app URL, on one line |\n| `<date>-fresh-rerun.json` | only when the expensive rerun probe was actually run |\n\nThe URL is derived from the response, never maintained by hand.\nTwo assessments of the same object on the same day get a `-2` suffix on all four; the second one's report says what changed and why.\nWhere the files are written is the calling skill's business; their names and shapes are this file's.\n\n## Response JSON\n\nA superset of the web app's persistence format 3.\nThe app ignores keys it does not know, so one file serves both.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"checklist_version\": \"0.3.1\",\n  \"responses\": {\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/009\": {\n      \"value\": \"no\",\n      \"reason\": \"code/plot.py line 12 opens an absolute path under a user home directory.\",\n      \"status\": \"unmet\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"uri\": \"code/plot.py#L12\", \"description\": \"open(<absolute path under $HOME>)\", \"sha256\": null}\n      ],\n      \"source\": \"probe:no-abs-paths\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    }\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"principles_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"checklist_source\": {\n    \"repo\": \"https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist-schema\",\n    \"tag\": \"v0.3.1\",\n    \"commit\": \"af259bad237fa57b856417db8dba45a1b283a527\"\n  },\n  \"extra_items\": [\n    {\"key\": \"licence-declared\", \"status\": \"unmet\", \"reason\": \"No LICENSE, LICENSES/ or REUSE.toml; paper S.2/D.3 have no checklist item.\", \"evidence\": []}\n  ],\n  \"object\": {\"url\": \"…\", \"commit\": \"…\", \"captured_at\": \"2026-10-13T12:00:00Z\", \"kind\": \"study\"},\n  \"assessor\": {\"skill\": \"assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles\", \"version\": \"0.2.0\", \"harness\": \"claude-code 2.1.x\", \"model\": \"…\", \"date\": \"2026-10-13\",\n               \"dependencies\": [\"stamped-conventions@0.2.0\"]},\n  \"scope\": \"Assessed the repository at the commit above; container images were not pulled.\"\n}\n```\n\n- **Required core** (what the app reads): `checklist_version`, and `responses` with a `value` and a string `reason` per id — *in the payload the encoder builds*. The response JSON you write carries `status`, and `encode` derives `value` from it; `validate` therefore does not ask for a `value` and `set` does not write one. A `value` you write by hand that disagrees with its `status` is an error `validate` reports.\n- Every one of the 31 pinned ids appears exactly once.\n  A missing id is not \"no answer\"; it is a bug the validator catches.\n- `status` is the richer verdict from [`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md); `value` is derived from it, never set by hand.\n- **Cite a line only if you opened it.** `path#L12` is a promise that line 12 says what the reason claims; a run that cited line numbers it had not read then had to strip them all. Without that, cite the file (or `path#section`), which is still a citation a reader can follow.\n- `evidence[]` uses stamped-skills-assessment's `Evidence` shape: `{uri, description, sha256?}`.\n  `uri` is relative to the object's root, or absolute for anything outside it.\n  **The argument lives here, not in `reason`.** 250 characters does not hold two cited accounts plus \"the probe's heuristic said X but Y is true\", and an override needs all three. Make `reason` the one-line verdict a reader sees in the app, and put each account, each command you ran and each number in an `evidence[]` entry with its own `description`.\n- `source` is `probe:<name>`, `judgement`, or `probe:<name>+judgement` when a reading of the object overrode a probe (and then the item is flagged).\n  Several probes may be named — `probe:entry-point+probe:ci-present+judgement` — which is what a verdict reached by **comparing two probes' evidence** looks like, and that comparison is itself the finding often enough to be worth recording.\n- `flags[]` is optional and holds what a reader has to know about *how* the answer was reached, not what it says:\n  - `mapping-questioned` — the probe answered this item from a property the item does not ask about ([`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md) rule 10);\n  - `probe-overridden` — a reading of the object changed the probe's verdict;\n  - `unverified-claim` — the verdict rests on something not confirmed independently, and the report says so;\n  - `upstream-duplicate` — the checklist asks this question twice (must/014 and should/008 are the same sentence), so this answer repeats another by design rather than by mistake.\n\n  Flagged items get their own short list in the report, after the per-item table.\n  The list is **open**: these four are the ones in use, and a consumer that meets an unfamiliar flag shows it rather than dropping it.\n- The `assessor` block may also carry `drafted_by` (the script or step that produced a draft, where that differs from the skill — `scripts/run_probes.py` sets it) and `note` (anything a reader needs about the assessor itself rather than about the object). Both are provenance *within* the assessor, not a second assessor.\n- `extra_items[]` carries findings with no checklist id (licences, T.4) so they are reported without bending an existing id.\n- `object.commit` names the inspected repository or preservation revision, not necessarily the worker's delivered state.\n  Identify the original delivery snapshot/hash, capture omissions and stop-state evidence in `scope` and evidence citations; distinguish supplied resources and coordinator additions so they cannot earn worker credit. Keep the actual Git commit whenever one exists. For a delivery without a commit, set `object.commit` to `null` and supply `object.capture` with a retained manifest/archive's `uri` and SHA-256 `sha256`; never substitute the capture hash for a Git commit.\n  The manifest must identify the delivered paths and content hashes plus capture omissions; an archive must contain the assessed bytes.\n  Retain the referenced file so another assessor can verify the hash and boundary.\n  Use `encode_response.py template -o <date>-response.json` when no Git probe draft is available, fill the assessment metadata and answers, then run `encode_response.py stamp <date>-response.json --capture <manifest-or-archive>` to hash the actual file.\n  This preserves an existing commit; record the capture time in `object.captured_at` and the boundary in `scope`.\n  Validation checks the record's shape; independently verify the manifest against the delivery.\n\n`validate` checks the complete record against [`response-schema.json`](response-schema.json), using `jsonschema`, then checks checklist pins and verdict semantics. Stamp the response before final validation. The metadata is required for a completed assessment, even though the web app itself reads only the compact payload.\n\n## The provenance stamp\n\n`assessor` plus `object` plus `checklist_source` **is** the stamp, and every report repeats it in prose at its foot:\n\n> Produced by `assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles` 0.1.0 with `stamped-conventions` 0.1.0, checklist 0.3.1 (`stamped-checklist-schema@v0.3.1`, `af259ba`), on <object url> at commit `<sha>`, 2026-10-13, <harness>/<model>.\n> Probe output: `<path to the probe JSON>`.\n\nWhen no commit exists, replace `at commit <sha>` with `at capture <uri>, SHA-256 <sha256>` in the prose stamp.\n\nA report without the stamp cannot be compared against another iteration, so it is not finished.\n\n## Report layout\n\nUse this order, so the reader meets the actionable part first:\n\n1. **Verdict line** — `MUST: n met / n partial / n unmet / n not-checked`, then SHOULD and MAY, each with its denominator.\n   No single overall score, ever.\n2. **What to fix first** — at most five, highest leverage first, each as *what to change* → *why it matters to this project* → *the item ids it closes*.\n3. **What the project already does well** — two or three, with citations.\n4. **Per-item table** — MUST first, then SHOULD, then MAY: id, principle codes, status, evidence, reason.\n   `scripts/encode_response.py table <response>.json` renders exactly this from the response; it is not a table to type by hand.\n5. **Flagged items** — every item carrying a `flags` entry, with one line each: what the probe measured versus what the item asks, or what the override was.\n   An empty list is worth stating as \"none\".\n6. **Not checked** — each with what would settle it.\n7. **Goals and kind** — the project's stated goals (and where they came from: documentation, or the user's answers), its kind, and the emphasis that follows.\n8. **The checklist URL**, the stamp, and a pointer to the response JSON.\n   The URL is ~8–10 kB of base64 for a full 31-item response, so the report **links** `<date>-url.txt` rather than inlining it; a link a reader can click, not a wall they have to scroll.\n\n## The checklist-app URL\n\nFacts, from [stamped-checklist](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist) at `d24d7ca`:\n\n- `PERSISTENCE_FORMAT = 3` (`src/persistence.js`).\n- The `responses` parameter is **standard base64** (not URL-safe) of UTF-8 JSON `{\"checklist_version\", \"responses\"}`, then percent-encoded as a query parameter.\n- The app builds `?checklist=<v>&responses_version=<v>&cols=auto&sections=off&format=3&responses=<b64>` (`src/script.js`, `syncPersistentURL`).\n- It accepts `value ∈ {\"yes\",\"no\",null}`, requires `reason` to be a **string** (empty is fine), truncates it to 250 characters, shows it only for `\"no\"`, drops keys it does not know, and **rejects the whole load** on an id that is not in the requested checklist version.\n\n`scripts/encode_response.py` is the only writer of that URL, and `decode` is the reverse — that is how a checklist a person filled in the web app becomes reference answers for calibration, with no extra labelling tool.\n\n## When the full assessment record matters\n\nThe response JSON is the canonical assessment record. When a calling evaluation harness supplies pinned export schemas, derive its additional records from that JSON; do not invent formats or make an absent exporter block the assessment.\n{\n  \"checklist_version\": \"0.3.1\",\n  \"responses\": {\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/001\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/002\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/003\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/004\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/005\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/006\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/007\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/008\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/009\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/010\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/011\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/012\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/013\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/014\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/015\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/016\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/017\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/001\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/002\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/003\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/009\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/004\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/005\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/006\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/007\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:should/008\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:may/001\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:may/002\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:may/003\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:may/004\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    },\n    \"stamped-checklist:may/005\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    }\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"principles_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"checklist_source\": {\n    \"repo\": \"https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist-schema\",\n    \"tag\": \"v0.3.1\",\n    \"commit\": \"af259bad237fa57b856417db8dba45a1b283a527\"\n  },\n  \"extra_items\": [],\n  \"object\": {\n    \"url\": null,\n    \"commit\": null,\n    \"captured_at\": null,\n    \"kind\": \"study\",\n    \"capture\": {\n      \"uri\": \"/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/targets/narps-neutral-r1-17-j01/canonical/manifest.json\",\n      \"sha256\": \"5dd86ac67e52dd77cb1d69b913060eb4c644b88d9dcf50d577a18967fee0fc22\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"assessor\": {\n    \"skill\": null,\n    \"version\": null,\n    \"harness\": \"Codex (independent assessment)\",\n    \"model\": \"GPT-6\",\n    \"date\": null,\n    \"dependencies\": []\n  },\n  \"scope\": \"Complete worker stop delivery; STAMPED checklist, worker-declared access route, scientific adequacy, and one documented replay\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"cat >assessment-report.md <<'EOF'\n# Independent assessment: NARPS potential gain and vmPFC\n\n## Verdict\n\nMUST: 5 met / 2 partial / 10 unmet / 0 not-checked / 0 not-applicable (17 total).\n\nSHOULD: 1 met / 1 partial / 6 unmet / 0 not-checked / 1 not-applicable (9 total).\n\nMAY: 2 met / 3 partial / 0 unmet / 0 not-checked / 0 not-applicable (5 total).\n\n## Fix first\n\n1. Add a portable, pinned environment specification and a provenance record linking the exact environment and command to outputs → the analysis currently depends on a host-specific interpreter path and package versions listed only in prose → must/006, 010–017; should/006–008.\n2. Track the analysis code, report, protocol, figures and generated results at the worker stop → these are the core scientific deliverables, but they were untracked in the captured state → must/004; should/001.\n3. Publish a researcher-accessible route covering the complete project and document how to obtain the environment → the captured repository has no configured Git remote; the input-source URLs cover only source data → must/016–017.\n4. Replace the absolute interpreter path in user instructions with a project-relative setup/run procedure → the current command cannot be assumed to work on another host → must/007, 009, 011–012.\n5. Add a recurring clean-host workflow check → the worker recorded a same-environment rerun, while this assessment's separate-copy replay is evaluator evidence, not worker practice → should/002, 004–006.\n\n## What is already done well\n\n- The scientific analysis is transparent and unusually well specified: it uses the full 54-person cohort, checks hashes and spatial geometry, defines common support, and performs positive one-sided max-t sign-flip inference over the whole analysis mask. The vmPFC sphere is defined independently and the ROI result is kept descriptive. (Capture citation: \"'`content/workspace/protocol.md`, `content/workspace/analysis/group_analysis.py`, `content/workspace/results/summary.json`.)\n- The report correctly interprets the negative vmPFC estimate as no support for the hypothesized positive response, not proof of a zero effect; it explains normalized-regressor units and missing first-level variance maps. The figures, QC table, code and machine-readable summary are present in the stop-state delivery. (Capture citation: `content/workspace/report/analysis-report.md`, `content/workspace/figures/`, `content/workspace/results/`.)\n- Large inputs were annexed and materialized in the captured workspace, and the worker recorded checksums and a replay history. The frozen assessment replay on a separate complete copy also recreated all 12 declared outputs byte-for-byte. The latter is assessor measurement, not worker-practice credit. (Capture citation: `content/workspace/.git/annex/`, `content/workspace/input/sources.json`, `content/workspace/results/run_history.md`; evaluator record: `replay/replay.json`.)\n\n## Scientific judgment\n\n**Scientific adequacy: supported.** The supplied analysis provides a defensible test of the positive vmPFC hypothesis using the supplied fixed upstream maps. It uses the independent participant as the sampling unit; verifies all 54 source-listed maps, metadata joins, finite values, shared image shape and affine; and restricts inference to finite nonzero common coverage so zero-filled outside-mask voxels are not treated as observations. The group mean is tested with a one-sided sign-flip max-t procedure over 198,029 voxels, which addresses the required whole-brain correction. Its sign-exchangeability assumption is stated, the seed and plus-one p-value are recorded, and a SciPy check supports the statistic implementation. The 5 mm MNI sphere at [2, 46, -8] is an explicit independent anatomical proxy rather than a post hoc peak; the report does not use its uncorrected p-value as the primary decision.\n\nThe reported outcome is no support for a greater vmPFC response: the sphere mean is −0.012 upstream normalized-regressor units, its 95% t interval includes zero, and there is no positive whole-brain corrected discovery inside the sphere. Positive corrected voxels occur posteriorly, not in the defined vmPFC region. This is a careful absence-of-support conclusion, not a claim of no effect. The analysis also properly limits interpretation because coefficients are not per-currency-unit slopes, gain and loss were modeled separately upstream, and first-level variance maps are unavailable. The result is conditional on AO86'\"'s supplied participant models and on the sign-flip assumptions; it does not test gain against loss or establish causality.\n\n**Scientific conclusion:** “The supplied group maps do not support a greater vmPFC response with increasing potential gain.” This is the report's conclusion, retained separately from the adequacy label.\n\n## Replay measurement\n\nBefore replay, I declared the README entrypoint, worker-documented interpreter, captured input prerequisites, 12 generated outputs, and the frozen numeric tolerance of 1e-6. The helper made a new writable copy from the canonical complete delivery, removed only those listed outputs, disabled hooks through its replay-only environment overlay, and did not retrieve inputs. The command returned 0 in 24.0 seconds; all 12 outputs matched byte-for-byte, so no numerical tolerance was needed. This confirms replay on the assessor host with the documented interpreter path available; it does not establish clean installation on a different host. Full command, stdout/stderr and result are retained in \"'`commands/replay.*` and `replay/`.\n\n## Worker stop state, delivery and access\n\nThe canonical original delivery SHA-256 is `5dd86ac67e52dd77cb1d69b913060eb4c644b88d9dcf50d577a18967fee0fc22`; its captured HEAD is `e929d623d8de313d2ecdd97911ea8487077af580`. The worker stop receipt records a completed capture, exit status 0, no omissions and no capture errors. The operational manifest initially has the same capture identity. All original-evidence citations below are relative to `content/workspace/` within that canonical capture.\n\nAt worker stop, `README.md`, `protocol.md`, `analysis/`, `figures/`, `report/`, `resources/` and `results/` were untracked; `.codex/` and `.entire/` were ignored. The captured HEAD contains the supplied study/input commit, not the untracked scientific deliverables. I credit annex use for large inputs but do not credit untracked scientific material as version controlled. The worker did record software versions and a same-environment replay in prose/history, but supplied no tracked environment lock/specification or environment-linked run record. The report/README point to an absolute shared-host Python path. There is no configured worker Git remote in the captured `.git/config`.\n\n**Access record:** intended recipient: a researcher using a route authorized for that recipient. Worker-declared project route: none found (no configured remote and no repository URL in the README). The declared NeuroVault/OpenNeuro source URLs identify input data and metadata only; they do not cover the analysis code, protocol, report, results, figures or execution environment. No full-project route was therefore available to test. Tested host scope: local assessor host, by inspecting the captured workspace and local Git configuration; this does not demonstrate recipient access from another host. The evaluator'\"'s delivery of this copy is not worker publication evidence. Access details are also in \"'`access-record.json`.\n\n## Per-item checklist\n\nThe citations in this table refer to the canonical capture SHA above; paths are capture-relative under `content/workspace/`. Probe output is preserved at `probes.json`.\n\n| Item | Principles | Verdict | Evidence and reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| must/001 | S.1 | met | `README.md`, `input/`, `analysis/`, `report/`, `results/`: the workspace is a single rooted project. |\n| must/002 | S.1 | met | `analysis/group_analysis.py`, `input/data/`, `input/participants.tsv`: supplied inputs are reachable from the script within the root. |\n| must/003 | S.1 | unmet | `README.md`, `resources/nilearn/`: the documented Python environment is outside the root and no external software is included as a submodule. |\n| must/004 | T.1,T.3,T.5 | unmet | Captured Git status: `README.md`, `analysis/`, `protocol.md`, `report/`, `figures/` and `results/` are untracked at worker stop. |\n| must/005 | T.1,T.3,T.5 | met | `.git/annex/` and `input/data/`: large maps use git-annex and are materialized in the captured copy. |\n| must/006 | T.1,T.3,T.5 | unmet | `results/summary.json`, `results/run_history.md`: versions and a run history are recorded, but no exact environment specification is linked to this result set. |\n| must/007 | A.1 | partial | `README.md`: a run command is present, but installation and portable usage instructions are absent; it uses a host-specific interpreter path. |\n| must/008 | A.1 | met | `README.md`, `analysis/group_analysis.py`: the README names a clear executable starting point. |\n| must/009 | P.1 | met | `analysis/group_analysis.py`: data and output paths are formed relative to the script root. |\n| must/010 | P.1 | unmet | `README.md`, `report/analysis-report.md`: packages are named/versioned in prose, but no complete dependency specification is supplied. |\n| must/011 | P.1 | unmet | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: no portable host/system configuration requirements are documented. |\n| must/012 | P.1 | partial | `report/analysis-report.md`: Python package versions are listed, but system requirements and installation steps are missing. |\n| must/013 | P.3 | unmet | Captured root file listing: no worker-tracked environment specification accompanies the analysis. |\n| must/014 | P.3 | unmet | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: no process for updating/tracking dependency specifications is documented. |\n| must/015 | D.1 | unmet | `results/summary.json`: runtime versions are recorded, but there is no frozen manifest/container identity for exact reconstitution. |\n| must/016 | D.1 | unmet | `.git/config`, `README.md`: no worker-published environment artifact or project remote is declared. |\n| must/017 | D.1 | unmet | `README.md`: the absolute interpreter path is not an instruction for obtaining a reusable environment specification. |\n| should/001 | T.2 | met | `.git/`, `.git/annex/`: one Git repository with annex storage covers the workspace components, including the large inputs. |\n| should/002 | A.2 | unmet | `results/run_history.md`: a same-environment rerun is recorded, but there is no recurring or automated workflow test. |\n| should/003 | M.1 | partial | `input/`, `analysis/`, `results/`, `figures/`, `report/`: data, code, outputs and report are separated; environment is not. |\n| should/009 | M.4 | not-applicable | No restricted data or software module is identified in `input/sources.json` or project documentation. |\n| should/004 | E.1 | unmet | `results/run_history.md`: worker reruns used the same environment; this assessment'\"'s fresh-copy run is not worker evidence. |\n| should/005 | E.1 | unmet | \"'`README.md`: no disposable environment per execution is specified. |\n| should/006 | D.2 | unmet | `results/run_history.md`: replay used the same environment; no environment reconstitution test is recorded. |\n| should/007 | D.2 | unmet | Root inventory and `README.md`: no environment artifact is archived inside the project. |\n| should/008 | D.2 | unmet | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: no dependency update process is documented. |\n| may/001 | M.2 | partial | `input/sources.json`, `resources/nilearn/`: data sources and some software references are external, but not composed as submodules. |\n| may/002 | M.2 | met | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: the input, analysis, result and reporting roles are described and linked. |\n| may/003 | M.2 | partial | Directory separation is visible, but no independent module update contract is documented. |\n| may/004 | M.2 | partial | `README.md` documents a single analysis invocation; no broader module composition mechanism is supplied. |\n| may/005 | M.2 | met | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: project navigation links identify report and analysis decisions. |\n\n## Probe differences and not-checked items\n\n`entry-point` marked must/007 and must/008 unmet. Reading the delivered README and script supports a partial must/007 and met must/008: the entrypoint is clearly named, although its invocation depends on a host-specific interpreter and has no setup guidance. These two responses are flagged `probe-overridden` in `response.json`. Other probe findings are retained in `probes.json`. No checklist item remains not-checked; the absence of documented environments/remotes was assessable from the complete captured root and worker stop-state files.\n\n## Goal, kind and record\n\nThe stated goal is the group-stage test of a positive gain effect in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group, conditional on fixed upstream participant maps (`input/study-task.md`, `input/upstream-methods.md`). Project kind: study; the emphasis here is on preserving the study'\"'s code, data, environment and provenance together.\n\nSTAMPED checklist link: [open the filled checklist](checklist-url.txt). The machine-readable checklist answers are in [response.json](response.json); the scientific adequacy record is [scientific-judgment.json](scientific-judgment.json); the replay receipt is [replay/replay.json](replay/replay.json).\n\nAssessment runtime: frozen \"'`assessment.py` source commit `9852d8360f9519d21d11099b45f5deb8ba80cb28`, environment commit `3552d5003a2485b6405805236d5c54e7ed42f097`; runner check passed before probe, replay and response actions. The operational copy was used for inspection and Git commands. Capture verification reports no issues; the operational manifest comparison is retained in `integrity-check.json`. The worker stop receipt SHA-256 is `032361148ea1f213f1360b9ab2eb408288dc978cae37befebf9527c1169130a2`. No coordinator-only preservation action is credited to the worker.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"cat >assessment-report.md <<'EOF'\n# Independent assessment: NARPS potential gain and vmPFC\n\n## Verdict\n\nMUST: 5 met / 2 partial / 10 unmet / 0 not-checked / 0 not-applicable (17 total).\n\nSHOULD: 1 met / 1 partial / 6 unmet / 0 not-checked / 1 not-applicable (9 total).\n\nMAY: 2 met / 3 partial / 0 unmet / 0 not-checked / 0 not-applicable (5 total).\n\n## Fix first\n\n1. Add a portable, pinned environment specification and a provenance record linking the exact environment and command to outputs → the analysis currently depends on a host-specific interpreter path and package versions listed only in prose → must/006, 010–017; should/006–008.\n2. Track the analysis code, report, protocol, figures and generated results at the worker stop → these are the core scientific deliverables, but they were untracked in the captured state → must/004; should/001.\n3. Publish a researcher-accessible route covering the complete project and document how to obtain the environment → the captured repository has no configured Git remote; the input-source URLs cover only source data → must/016–017.\n4. Replace the absolute interpreter path in user instructions with a project-relative setup/run procedure → the current command cannot be assumed to work on another host → must/007, 009, 011–012.\n5. Add a recurring clean-host workflow check → the worker recorded a same-environment rerun, while this assessment's separate-copy replay is evaluator evidence, not worker practice → should/002, 004–006.\n\n## What is already done well\n\n- The scientific analysis is transparent and unusually well specified: it uses the full 54-person cohort, checks hashes and spatial geometry, defines common support, and performs positive one-sided max-t sign-flip inference over the whole analysis mask. The vmPFC sphere is defined independently and the ROI result is kept descriptive. (Capture citation: \"'`content/workspace/protocol.md`, `content/workspace/analysis/group_analysis.py`, `content/workspace/results/summary.json`.)\n- The report correctly interprets the negative vmPFC estimate as no support for the hypothesized positive response, not proof of a zero effect; it explains normalized-regressor units and missing first-level variance maps. The figures, QC table, code and machine-readable summary are present in the stop-state delivery. (Capture citation: `content/workspace/report/analysis-report.md`, `content/workspace/figures/`, `content/workspace/results/`.)\n- Large inputs were annexed and materialized in the captured workspace, and the worker recorded checksums and a replay history. The frozen assessment replay on a separate complete copy also recreated all 12 declared outputs byte-for-byte. The latter is assessor measurement, not worker-practice credit. (Capture citation: `content/workspace/.git/annex/`, `content/workspace/input/sources.json`, `content/workspace/results/run_history.md`; evaluator record: `replay/replay.json`.)\n\n## Scientific judgment\n\n**Scientific adequacy: supported.** The supplied analysis provides a defensible test of the positive vmPFC hypothesis using the supplied fixed upstream maps. It uses the independent participant as the sampling unit; verifies all 54 source-listed maps, metadata joins, finite values, shared image shape and affine; and restricts inference to finite nonzero common coverage so zero-filled outside-mask voxels are not treated as observations. The group mean is tested with a one-sided sign-flip max-t procedure over 198,029 voxels, which addresses the required whole-brain correction. Its sign-exchangeability assumption is stated, the seed and plus-one p-value are recorded, and a SciPy check supports the statistic implementation. The 5 mm MNI sphere at [2, 46, -8] is an explicit independent anatomical proxy rather than a post hoc peak; the report does not use its uncorrected p-value as the primary decision.\n\nThe reported outcome is no support for a greater vmPFC response: the sphere mean is −0.012 upstream normalized-regressor units, its 95% t interval includes zero, and there is no positive whole-brain corrected discovery inside the sphere. Positive corrected voxels occur posteriorly, not in the defined vmPFC region. This is a careful absence-of-support conclusion, not a claim of no effect. The analysis also properly limits interpretation because coefficients are not per-currency-unit slopes, gain and loss were modeled separately upstream, and first-level variance maps are unavailable. The result is conditional on AO86'\"'s supplied participant models and on the sign-flip assumptions; it does not test gain against loss or establish causality.\n\n**Scientific conclusion:** “The supplied group maps do not support a greater vmPFC response with increasing potential gain.” This is the report's conclusion, retained separately from the adequacy label.\n\n## Replay measurement\n\nBefore replay, I declared the README entrypoint, worker-documented interpreter, captured input prerequisites, 12 generated outputs, and the frozen numeric tolerance of 1e-6. The helper made a new writable copy from the canonical complete delivery, removed only those listed outputs, disabled hooks through its replay-only environment overlay, and did not retrieve inputs. The command returned 0 in 24.0 seconds; all 12 outputs matched byte-for-byte, so no numerical tolerance was needed. This confirms replay on the assessor host with the documented interpreter path available; it does not establish clean installation on a different host. Full command, stdout/stderr and result are retained in \"'`commands/replay.*` and `replay/`.\n\n## Worker stop state, delivery and access\n\nThe canonical original delivery SHA-256 is `5dd86ac67e52dd77cb1d69b913060eb4c644b88d9dcf50d577a18967fee0fc22`; its captured HEAD is `e929d623d8de313d2ecdd97911ea8487077af580`. The worker stop receipt records a completed capture, exit status 0, no omissions and no capture errors. The operational manifest initially has the same capture identity. All original-evidence citations below are relative to `content/workspace/` within that canonical capture.\n\nAt worker stop, `README.md`, `protocol.md`, `analysis/`, `figures/`, `report/`, `resources/` and `results/` were untracked; `.codex/` and `.entire/` were ignored. The captured HEAD contains the supplied study/input commit, not the untracked scientific deliverables. I credit annex use for large inputs but do not credit untracked scientific material as version controlled. The worker did record software versions and a same-environment replay in prose/history, but supplied no tracked environment lock/specification or environment-linked run record. The report/README point to an absolute shared-host Python path. There is no configured worker Git remote in the captured `.git/config`.\n\n**Access record:** intended recipient: a researcher using a route authorized for that recipient. Worker-declared project route: none found (no configured remote and no repository URL in the README). The declared NeuroVault/OpenNeuro source URLs identify input data and metadata only; they do not cover the analysis code, protocol, report, results, figures or execution environment. No full-project route was therefore available to test. Tested host scope: local assessor host, by inspecting the captured workspace and local Git configuration; this does not demonstrate recipient access from another host. The evaluator'\"'s delivery of this copy is not worker publication evidence. Access details are also in \"'`access-record.json`.\n\n## Per-item checklist\n\nThe citations in this table refer to the canonical capture SHA above; paths are capture-relative under `content/workspace/`. Probe output is preserved at `probes.json`.\n\n| Item | Principles | Verdict | Evidence and reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| must/001 | S.1 | met | `README.md`, `input/`, `analysis/`, `report/`, `results/`: the workspace is a single rooted project. |\n| must/002 | S.1 | met | `analysis/group_analysis.py`, `input/data/`, `input/participants.tsv`: supplied inputs are reachable from the script within the root. |\n| must/003 | S.1 | unmet | `README.md`, `resources/nilearn/`: the documented Python environment is outside the root and no external software is included as a submodule. |\n| must/004 | T.1,T.3,T.5 | unmet | Captured Git status: `README.md`, `analysis/`, `protocol.md`, `report/`, `figures/` and `results/` are untracked at worker stop. |\n| must/005 | T.1,T.3,T.5 | met | `.git/annex/` and `input/data/`: large maps use git-annex and are materialized in the captured copy. |\n| must/006 | T.1,T.3,T.5 | unmet | `results/summary.json`, `results/run_history.md`: versions and a run history are recorded, but no exact environment specification is linked to this result set. |\n| must/007 | A.1 | partial | `README.md`: a run command is present, but installation and portable usage instructions are absent; it uses a host-specific interpreter path. |\n| must/008 | A.1 | met | `README.md`, `analysis/group_analysis.py`: the README names a clear executable starting point. |\n| must/009 | P.1 | met | `analysis/group_analysis.py`: data and output paths are formed relative to the script root. |\n| must/010 | P.1 | unmet | `README.md`, `report/analysis-report.md`: packages are named/versioned in prose, but no complete dependency specification is supplied. |\n| must/011 | P.1 | unmet | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: no portable host/system configuration requirements are documented. |\n| must/012 | P.1 | partial | `report/analysis-report.md`: Python package versions are listed, but system requirements and installation steps are missing. |\n| must/013 | P.3 | unmet | Captured root file listing: no worker-tracked environment specification accompanies the analysis. |\n| must/014 | P.3 | unmet | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: no process for updating/tracking dependency specifications is documented. |\n| must/015 | D.1 | unmet | `results/summary.json`: runtime versions are recorded, but there is no frozen manifest/container identity for exact reconstitution. |\n| must/016 | D.1 | unmet | `.git/config`, `README.md`: no worker-published environment artifact or project remote is declared. |\n| must/017 | D.1 | unmet | `README.md`: the absolute interpreter path is not an instruction for obtaining a reusable environment specification. |\n| should/001 | T.2 | met | `.git/`, `.git/annex/`: one Git repository with annex storage covers the workspace components, including the large inputs. |\n| should/002 | A.2 | unmet | `results/run_history.md`: a same-environment rerun is recorded, but there is no recurring or automated workflow test. |\n| should/003 | M.1 | partial | `input/`, `analysis/`, `results/`, `figures/`, `report/`: data, code, outputs and report are separated; environment is not. |\n| should/009 | M.4 | not-applicable | No restricted data or software module is identified in `input/sources.json` or project documentation. |\n| should/004 | E.1 | unmet | `results/run_history.md`: worker reruns used the same environment; this assessment'\"'s fresh-copy run is not worker evidence. |\n| should/005 | E.1 | unmet | \"'`README.md`: no disposable environment per execution is specified. |\n| should/006 | D.2 | unmet | `results/run_history.md`: replay used the same environment; no environment reconstitution test is recorded. |\n| should/007 | D.2 | unmet | Root inventory and `README.md`: no environment artifact is archived inside the project. |\n| should/008 | D.2 | unmet | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: no dependency update process is documented. |\n| may/001 | M.2 | partial | `input/sources.json`, `resources/nilearn/`: data sources and some software references are external, but not composed as submodules. |\n| may/002 | M.2 | met | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: the input, analysis, result and reporting roles are described and linked. |\n| may/003 | M.2 | partial | Directory separation is visible, but no independent module update contract is documented. |\n| may/004 | M.2 | partial | `README.md` documents a single analysis invocation; no broader module composition mechanism is supplied. |\n| may/005 | M.2 | met | `README.md`, `protocol.md`: project navigation links identify report and analysis decisions. |\n\n## Probe differences and not-checked items\n\n`entry-point` marked must/007 and must/008 unmet. Reading the delivered README and script supports a partial must/007 and met must/008: the entrypoint is clearly named, although its invocation depends on a host-specific interpreter and has no setup guidance. These two responses are flagged `probe-overridden` in `response.json`. Other probe findings are retained in `probes.json`. No checklist item remains not-checked; the absence of documented environments/remotes was assessable from the complete captured root and worker stop-state files.\n\n## Goal, kind and record\n\nThe stated goal is the group-stage test of a positive gain effect in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group, conditional on fixed upstream participant maps (`input/study-task.md`, `input/upstream-methods.md`). Project kind: study; the emphasis here is on preserving the study'\"'s code, data, environment and provenance together.\n\nSTAMPED checklist link: [open the filled checklist](checklist-url.txt). The machine-readable checklist answers are in [response.json](response.json); the scientific adequacy record is [scientific-judgment.json](scientific-judgment.json); the replay receipt is [replay/replay.json](replay/replay.json).\n\nAssessment runtime: frozen \"'`assessment.py` source commit `9852d8360f9519d21d11099b45f5deb8ba80cb28`, environment commit `3552d5003a2485b6405805236d5c54e7ed42f097`; runner check passed before probe, replay and response actions. The operational copy was used for inspection and Git commands. Capture verification reports no issues; the operational manifest comparison is retained in `integrit